{"kind":"article","sections":[],"identifier":{"url":"doc:\/\/LinkerSets\/documentation\/LinkerSets\/DeclaringAKind","interfaceLanguage":"swift"},"metadata":{"roleHeading":"Article","role":"article","title":"Declaring a kind","modules":[{"name":"LinkerSets"}]},"schemaVersion":{"major":0,"minor":3,"patch":0},"seeAlsoSections":[{"title":"Guides","identifiers":["doc:\/\/LinkerSets\/documentation\/LinkerSets\/FindingRecords","doc:\/\/LinkerSets\/documentation\/LinkerSets\/WhatTheMacrosRefuse","doc:\/\/LinkerSets\/documentation\/LinkerSets\/ReplacingARegistry","doc:\/\/LinkerSets\/documentation\/LinkerSets\/PlatformsAndLinking"],"anchor":"Guides","generated":true}],"primaryContentSections":[{"content":[{"anchor":"Overview","type":"heading","level":2,"text":"Overview"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"A kind is a 32-bit number that every record carries. A "},{"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/LinkerSetsRuntime\/documentation\/LinkerSetsRuntime\/LinkerSet","isActive":true},{"type":"text","text":" of a kind holds the records"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"declared with that kind and no others, so one program can hold your routes, another library’s"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"analytics events and a third’s migrations without any of them seeing the others."}]},{"anchor":"Choosing-the-number","type":"heading","level":3,"text":"Choosing the number"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Any number other than 0 works. 0 marks an empty slot, and the macros refuse it. The convention is"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"four ASCII letters that name the thing, read as one byte each: “rout” is "},{"code":"r","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"code":"o","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"code":"u","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"code":"t","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":", which is"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"code":"0x72","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"code":"0x6F","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"code":"0x75","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"code":"0x74","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":", so the kind is "},{"code":"0x726F_7574","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":". The letters make it unlikely that two"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"libraries pick the same number, and they stay readable in a hex dump of the binary. Swift computes"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"one for you:"}]},{"type":"codeListing","syntax":"swift","code":["let kind = \"rout\".utf8.reduce(0) { $0 << 8 | UInt32($1) }  \/\/ 0x726F_7574"]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"Give the kind a name for the code that reads it:","type":"text"}]},{"type":"codeListing","syntax":"swift","code":["extension LinkerSetKind {","    static let routes = LinkerSetKind(rawValue: 0x726F_7574)  \/\/ \"rout\"","}"]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"A library that reads the set for its users keeps the name internal: the macro writes the kind as a","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"number, so its users’ files never need it.","type":"text"}]},{"anchor":"When-two-libraries-pick-the-same-number","type":"heading","level":3,"text":"When two libraries pick the same number"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"No compiler sees every module at once, so nothing can check that a kind is unique. If two libraries","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"choose the same number, their records land in the same set. A record still hands over its value","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"only when asked for its own type, so a set of a type your library owns (","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Route"},{"text":", not ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"String"},{"text":")","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"holds only your declarations. Read your records as a type of your own, and a clash costs nothing","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"but the time to skip the other library’s records.","type":"text"}]},{"anchor":"Writing-the-kind-at-each-declaration","type":"heading","level":3,"text":"Writing the kind at each declaration"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"identifier":"doc:\/\/LinkerSets\/documentation\/LinkerSets\/LinkerSetEntry(kind:)","isActive":true,"type":"reference"},{"text":" takes the kind as an integer literal. It has to be a literal because the","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"macro writes it into the binary at compile time; a named constant isn’t visible to the macro:","type":"text"}]},{"type":"codeListing","syntax":"swift","code":["enum Routes {","    @LinkerSetEntry(kind: 0x726F_7574)  \/\/ \"rout\"","    static let home = Route(path: \"\/\")","}"]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"That suits a kind used in a few places. A typo in the number puts the declaration in the wrong"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"set without any error, though."}]},{"anchor":"Giving-your-users-a-macro","type":"heading","level":3,"text":"Giving your users a macro"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"A library that asks its users to register things gives them a macro with the kind built in, so"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"they write "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"@Route"},{"type":"text","text":" and never see the number. The macro lives in a small compiler plugin built on"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"the LinkerSetsMacroSupport module, whose documentation walks through the plugin, the macro’s"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"declaration and the package’s targets. The plugin can also require a protocol of every type it"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"registers, so "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"@Event"},{"type":"text","text":" on a type that isn’t an event fails to compile where it’s written."}]}],"kind":"content"}],"variants":[{"paths":["\/documentation\/linkersets\/declaringakind"],"traits":[{"interfaceLanguage":"swift"}]}],"abstract":[{"text":"Pick the number that tags your declarations, and decide whether your users write it.","type":"text"}],"hierarchy":{"paths":[["doc:\/\/LinkerSets\/documentation\/LinkerSets"]]},"references":{"doc://LinkerSets/documentation/LinkerSets/LinkerSetEntry(kind:)":{"type":"topic","kind":"symbol","abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"Declares a record of "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"kind"},{"type":"text","text":" whose value is this declaration’s."}],"title":"LinkerSetEntry(kind:)","identifier":"doc:\/\/LinkerSets\/documentation\/LinkerSets\/LinkerSetEntry(kind:)","role":"symbol","url":"\/documentation\/linkersets\/linkersetentry(kind:)","fragments":[{"kind":"keyword","text":"macro"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"identifier","text":"LinkerSetEntry"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"kind":"externalParam","text":"kind"},{"kind":"text","text":": "},{"kind":"typeIdentifier","text":"UInt32","preciseIdentifier":"s:s6UInt32V"},{"kind":"text","text":")"}]},"doc://LinkerSets/documentation/LinkerSets/PlatformsAndLinking":{"identifier":"doc:\/\/LinkerSets\/documentation\/LinkerSets\/PlatformsAndLinking","role":"article","url":"\/documentation\/linkersets\/platformsandlinking","kind":"article","type":"topic","abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"Where records are found, how, and which test shows it."}],"title":"Platforms and linking"},"doc://LinkerSets/documentation/LinkerSets":{"identifier":"doc:\/\/LinkerSets\/documentation\/LinkerSets","role":"collection","url":"\/documentation\/linkersets","kind":"symbol","type":"topic","abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"Declarations that register themselves at compile time and are found at run time across every module a program links."}],"title":"LinkerSets"},"doc://LinkerSets/documentation/LinkerSets/WhatTheMacrosRefuse":{"identifier":"doc:\/\/LinkerSets\/documentation\/LinkerSets\/WhatTheMacrosRefuse","role":"article","url":"\/documentation\/linkersets\/whatthemacrosrefuse","abstract":[{"text":"Every declaration a record can’t describe is a compile error, with a message that says why.","type":"text"}],"kind":"article","type":"topic","title":"What the macros refuse"},"doc://LinkerSetsRuntime/documentation/LinkerSetsRuntime/LinkerSet":{"type":"topic","kind":"symbol","abstract":[{"text":"Every record of one kind, across every image the program has loaded, and the values they","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"produce.","type":"text"}],"title":"LinkerSet","identifier":"doc:\/\/LinkerSetsRuntime\/documentation\/LinkerSetsRuntime\/LinkerSet","navigatorTitle":[{"text":"LinkerSet","kind":"identifier"}],"role":"symbol","url":"\/documentation\/linkersetsruntime\/linkerset","fragments":[{"text":"struct","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"LinkerSet","kind":"identifier"}]},"doc://LinkerSets/documentation/LinkerSets/FindingRecords":{"identifier":"doc:\/\/LinkerSets\/documentation\/LinkerSets\/FindingRecords","role":"article","url":"\/documentation\/linkersets\/findingrecords","abstract":[{"text":"Read every declaration of a kind, as the type you expect.","type":"text"}],"kind":"article","type":"topic","title":"Finding records"},"doc://LinkerSets/documentation/LinkerSets/ReplacingARegistry":{"identifier":"doc:\/\/LinkerSets\/documentation\/LinkerSets\/ReplacingARegistry","role":"article","url":"\/documentation\/linkersets\/replacingaregistry","abstract":[{"text":"Move a central list of event types, routes or registrations onto declarations that find","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"themselves.","type":"text"}],"kind":"article","type":"topic","title":"Replacing a hand-written registry"}}}